# Dev Configs & Dotfiles Kit

Finding your workflow setup means borrowing from people who've already solved the hard parts. Here's where to steal (the good kind).

---

## The Essentials

Three repos that cover everything:

- [Awesome VS Code Settings](https://github.com/palashmon/awesome-vscode-settings) — `settings.json` snippets for a frictionless editor
- [Dotfiles Meta-Index](https://dotfiles.github.io/) — How real developers structure their configs
- [Awesome VS Code](https://github.com/viatsko/awesome-vscode) — Themes, extensions, tools (the full directory)

---

## Repos to Reference

### [Awesome VS Code Settings](https://github.com/palashmon/awesome-vscode-settings)

**What**: Curated `settings.json` snippets that actually matter.

**Find here**: Smooth cursor animations, visual clutter hiding, optimized search exclusions, and the tiny tweaks that make your editor feel intentional.

### [Mathias's Dotfiles](https://github.com/mathiasbynens/dotfiles)

**What**: The canonical reference for dotfiles.

**Find here**: [Bash](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/), [Git](https://git-scm.com/), and sensible OS defaults. Half the internet forks this as their baseline.

### [Webpro's Awesome Dotfiles](https://github.com/webpro/awesome-dotfiles)

**What**: A meta-list of dotfile repos, organized by OS and shell.

**Find here**: Configs filtered by [Zsh](https://www.zsh.org/), [Fish](https://fishshell.com/), and more. Good for finding a template that matches your actual workflow.

